      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194531/assets/afeee9a0b7dd8a68368444ca475a6ed9493f0dfb007f4e8406b8fe2a32abf9e4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x}).$ What is the value of the constant $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the graph of $y=k({2}^{x})$ we take the graph of $y={2}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$\n\nComparing the point $(0,2)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=2.$",
